WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.989 --> 00:00:02.250
Hello Internet.

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:08.370
Welcome to Film Theory, the show that wishes
there was a Night MatPat to split the workload

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:14.050
no more FNAF
So Rick and Morty is about to return from

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:15.269
its midseason break.

00:00:15.269 --> 00:00:18.590
But before it does, I figured it was a good
time to take a look at the season's first

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:21.949
six episodes to check out where things might
be headed next.

00:00:21.949 --> 00:00:26.849
After ending season 5 by blowing a huge hole
into the universe, Rick and Morty opened season

00:00:26.849 --> 00:00:29.650
6 with an all new, all different mandate.

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:33.410
In our previous Rick and Morty theory, we
looked specifically at the first episode of

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:37.210
the season to conclude that it was all about
ways of handling grief.

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:41.350
Rick refuses to let go of his past trauma
enough so that he's torturing himself and

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:45.980
everyone around him with it.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:54.020
Meanwhile, the Jerry from Season one's Kronenberg
Universe completely lets go of his past trauma

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:58.770
to a self-destructive degree, refusing to
accept anyone into his life and severing all

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:05.930
ties with anyone from his past.

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:08.670
The true solution, however, is found somewhere
in the middle.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:13.530
Grief isn't solved by never moving on, but
it's also not solved by completely moving

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:14.530
on.

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:18.530
It's a process of slow healing, acknowledging
that that trauma is always going to be a part

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:20.780
of you and just learning to live with that.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:25.260
We concluded that this episode was setting
up a season-long theme of healing for Rick,

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:26.390
working on himself.

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:29.590
and boy howdy did we get that one right on
the money!

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:32.330
I haven't been more right about a theory since…

00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:33.330
ahh…

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:34.330
Mmm

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:38.070
In each of these first six episodes, we watch
Rick actively going out of his way to do the

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:41.860
most un-Rick thing ever, helping the family
members around him.

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:45.450
When Beth falls in love with a clone of herself
in episode three, we see him doing something

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:48.649
we've never seen him do before in the Infinite
Universes.

00:01:48.649 --> 00:01:53.330
Give fatherly advice.

00:01:53.330 --> 00:02:04.460
When Morty’s psyche is fragmented into a
billion pieces by a video game, Rick spends

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:08.270
a lifetime trying to put those pieces back
together, lowering his emotional walls to

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:17.849
try and help recover as much of Morty as he
can.

00:02:17.849 --> 00:02:24.640
Rick even decides to show Jerry some respect.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:29.430
Jerry!!

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:33.489
And when a fortune cookie dooms Jerry to a
fate worse than death, Rick even gives up

00:02:33.489 --> 00:02:37.110
universal power just to save him.

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:48.700
And while he may have lost immortal power,
he gained 

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:51.390
a friend.

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:55.319
All of this without the crutch of inter-dimensional
portal travel, despite Rick saying that he

00:02:55.319 --> 00:02:57.819
was working on fixing it throughout the season.

00:02:57.819 --> 00:03:00.110
But of course, nothing can last forever.

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:04.409
In the final episode, right before the midseason
break, Rick restores portal travel and promises

00:03:04.409 --> 00:03:11.470
to return to classic, wacky Rick and Morty
adventures.

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:19.549
So now this begs the question with Rick and
the Smiths well, on their way through the

00:03:19.549 --> 00:03:21.400
healing process, what comes next?

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:25.239
Are we leaving behind the canon in lore established
by the front half of the season and returning

00:03:25.239 --> 00:03:28.500
to completely disconnected episodic adventures,
like Rick says?

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:29.680
I don't think so.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:35.230
And it's all because of one simple truth,
healing leaves scars: physically, emotionally

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:36.230
and mentally.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:39.450
And it's time our characters start dealing
with those scars.

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:43.590
And of them, none is bigger than a certain
inter-dimensional world hopper set up in the

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:50.989
season's very first episode.

00:03:50.989 --> 00:03:51.989
That's right friends.

00:03:51.989 --> 00:03:56.080
By analyzing the season's episodes so far
and what they're saying about the show's characters.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:00.040
I believe that we can predict exactly where
Rick and Morty is headed for the rest of the

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:01.040
season.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:04.030
And while yeah, we're going to see the return
of some dimension hopping adventures across

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:05.030
space and time.

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:08.519
We're also going to be pulling on a story
thread that's been lingering over our heads

00:04:08.519 --> 00:04:10.420
for several seasons now.

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:15.379
Spoiler alert there's a Rick out there that's
going to shatter the status quo of this universe

00:04:15.379 --> 00:04:16.379
forever.

00:04:16.379 --> 00:04:20.290
So open up your emergency fortune cookie loyal
theorist, we're heading in.

00:04:20.290 --> 00:04:24.110
Now, obviously Rick healing the bonds with
his family was a huge part of these first

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:25.930
six episodes, but that wasn't all.

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:30.370
Another recurring theme that stood out across
the first half of the season was dissatisfaction.

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:34.280
Everyone who got what they thought they wanted
actually didn't end up all that happy about

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:35.280
it.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:39.630
Marta, the leader of the Morty religion inside
Morty's brain, manages to unite 92% of the

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:42.150
world to her cause, a monumental task.

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:46.340
But she's left unhappy with the reality that
not everyone in this video game simulation

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:47.790
is going to be united as one.

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:52.320
The nocturnal Smith Night Family manages to
overthrow the Day Smiths in a coup, thereby

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:54.070
allowing them to be in charge of their lives.

00:04:54.070 --> 00:04:58.430
But then they hate the responsibility that
comes with being active during the day, so

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:01.280
they voluntarily hand control back to the
Day Smiths.

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:05.710
When hyper intelligent space dinosaurs show
up on Earth to create a utopia for humanity,

00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:19.790
humans just become bored with this life without
struggle.

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:23.660
In every case, these characters are getting
exactly what they wanted, but they're left

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:25.610
empty by it in some way or another.

00:05:25.610 --> 00:05:26.610
So what does it mean?

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:31.170
if you're left unfulfilled after getting what
you want, what even is the purpose of life?

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:32.419
Is there a purpose to life?

00:05:32.419 --> 00:05:36.590
Well, that, my friends, brings us to a personal
favorite of the Rick and Morty franchise:

00:05:36.590 --> 00:05:38.900
Nihilism, the belief that nothing matters.

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:41.560
Now, this is really just boiling it down to
the bare bones.

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:46.220
But nihilists often believe that there's no
moral order, no actual fundamental purpose

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:48.800
to life or existence within the universe.

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:49.800
Again, nothing matters.

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:54.840
And it's this concept that almost perfectly
embodies Rick Sanchez, a man who has seen

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:59.169
infinity and has thus lost the ability to
care about finite things.

00:05:59.169 --> 00:06:03.470
Nothing matters because all the outcomes are
not only possible, they've already happened

00:06:03.470 --> 00:06:04.600
out there somewhere.

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:16.880
He's seen how pointless it is to try and solve
a problem.

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:20.630
So you could say that these characters getting
what they want and then finding out it didn't

00:06:20.630 --> 00:06:23.169
mean anything supports the view of nihilism.

00:06:23.169 --> 00:06:25.919
It proves that Rick Sanchez is right.

00:06:25.919 --> 00:06:28.470
But there are other ways to look at this philosophy.

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:31.889
There's a flipside here that views the pointless
universe positively.

00:06:31.889 --> 00:06:34.060
It's called optimistic nihilism.

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:37.470
If we truly live in a universe where nothing
matters, well, that's just another way of

00:06:37.470 --> 00:06:39.910
saying that everything matters equally.

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:42.960
Anything can matter as much as you decide
it matters.

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:46.720
You could, for instance, decide that this
channel matters more to you than anything

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:50.180
else in the universe, and then go and hit
that subscribe button below the video.

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:53.500
Under optimistic nihilism that is a perfectly
fair assessment.

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:57.120
And looking at the general ecosystem of YouTube…
yeah, I’d say it's not that far off.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:01.259
In the context of Rick and Morty: of all characters
in the show, Morty is the one who most closely

00:07:01.259 --> 00:07:02.539
represents this belief.

00:07:02.539 --> 00:07:10.710
He laid this all out way back in season one.

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:13.510
Rick's healing arc through the first half
of the season has shown him slowly coming

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:15.120
over to this exact idea.

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:19.009
He knows that scientifically nothing matters
because he's seen the infinite multiverse.

00:07:19.009 --> 00:07:23.630
But the people and events in his small corner
of the multiverse can matter.

00:07:23.630 --> 00:07:25.810
When Jerry gives Rick a friendship fortune
cookie.

00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:26.810
Sure.

00:07:26.810 --> 00:07:30.610
Rick calls this out as a stupid gesture, but
notice that he keeps the fortune.

00:07:30.610 --> 00:07:34.319
He even goes so far as to look at it again
before putting it in his pocket.

00:07:34.319 --> 00:07:36.810
It's a small gesture that meant something.

00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:40.870
With Beth constantly going through self-confidence
issues, Rick decides to leave out a special

00:07:40.870 --> 00:07:44.319
wine that helps her open up and discover herself
with Space Beth.

00:07:44.319 --> 00:07:48.419
And when a portion of Morty's psyche asks
that Rick keep their video game world on so

00:07:48.419 --> 00:08:01.430
they can continue to exist, Rick listens and
chooses to follow 

00:08:01.430 --> 00:08:02.430
that request.

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:04.650
He has no reason to do this.

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:08.909
He got exactly what he wanted out of the game
and has nothing to gain from being beholden

00:08:08.909 --> 00:08:10.530
to a video game character.

00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:13.650
And yet he keeps the game running because
it's a nice thing to do.

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:18.650
It's a small action that has a big impact
in the personal universe of someone else.

00:08:18.650 --> 00:08:21.729
It's what all of these situations in season
six have in common.

00:08:21.729 --> 00:08:26.700
He has nothing to gain, but he's using his
godlike powers to actually help his family.

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:30.370
Even back in episode one, Rick chooses to
rescue the Smiths instead of continuing his

00:08:30.370 --> 00:08:35.719
self-destructive pursuit of Rick Prime.

00:08:35.719 --> 00:08:40.529
And this positive attitude is starting to
leak into other parts of the show as well.

00:08:40.529 --> 00:08:44.100
Throughout the last five seasons, Rick has
gotten actively annoyed whenever the show

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:48.960
gets meta, calling attention to its own narrative
in order to play with the concept of how narrative

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:49.960
works.

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:53.580
That's what the whole Story Train episode
was about a satire on story structure in the

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:56.480
in universe consequences of long form storytelling.

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:00.720
He even has contempt for his own back story.

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:06.930
And really Rick's previous hatred for the
idea of continuity is just an extension of

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:08.000
his nihilism.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:12.850
If you believe that nothing matters well,
canon and lore directly conflict with that

00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:13.850
belief.

00:09:13.850 --> 00:09:17.330
But now, after healing and embracing this
more optimistic view of the world, Rick suddenly

00:09:17.330 --> 00:09:20.269
cares about the potential stories that his
series can explore.

00:09:20.269 --> 00:09:24.600
For example, when he's asked about the rift
left by last season's finale, he actively

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:30.790
defends its existence.

00:09:30.790 --> 00:09:35.860
Normally that's the sort of thing that would
drive him insane.

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:37.700
He’d despise the fact that it's there.

00:09:37.700 --> 00:09:41.530
And yet when the space dinosaurs sew the rift
shut Rick becomes furious about the apparent

00:09:41.530 --> 00:09:49.390
loss of potential stories.

00:09:49.390 --> 00:09:53.040
Continuity is slowly starting to matter to
him because it anchors him, it grounds him,

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:55.080
it gives him something to actually care about.

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:58.880
Sure, in the grand scheme it might be meaningless,
but to him it does have meaning.

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:03.400
But this sort of investment from characters
as powerful as Rick can have some major consequences.

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:07.850
In episode six, we meet the Space Dinosaurs,
super intelligent beings on par with Rick.

00:10:07.850 --> 00:10:19.190
These dinosaurs travel the universe to help
other species while doing sick ollies.

00:10:19.190 --> 00:10:23.680
But wherever the dinosaurs go to help meteors
follow, apparently a species of giant meteor

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:42.250
evolved to be their equal opposite, their
ideological nemesis.

00:10:42.250 --> 00:10:47.350
It's a force of absolute death and destruction
versus an equal and opposite force of creation

00:10:47.350 --> 00:10:48.350
and creativity.

00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:51.130
The more good the dinosaurs do and the more
successful they are, the more meteors are

00:10:51.130 --> 00:10:53.460
born to destroy their progress.

00:10:53.460 --> 00:10:54.460
And here's the thing.

00:10:54.460 --> 00:10:58.500
If this is indeed a rule of the universe,
enough so that it's happened several times

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:02.340
to the dinosaurs, this then also applies to
Rick.

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:06.850
After all, he and the Space Dinos already
see themselves as equals.

00:11:06.850 --> 00:11:13.120
So it would follow that Rick also has himself
an equal and opposite force, a force that's

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:21.040
been evolving alongside Rick in the opposite
direction.

00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:22.280
That's right, loyal theorist.

00:11:22.280 --> 00:11:26.339
I believe that Rick Prime, the character that
killed our Rick's family and started the entire

00:11:26.339 --> 00:11:31.370
chain of events that would lead to the series,
is not only our Rick's nemesis, but his universal

00:11:31.370 --> 00:11:32.400
counterweight.

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:33.400
Kind of.

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:37.290
It's actually more accurate to say that our
Rick is Rick Prime's meteor.

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:41.120
See, from what we can tell, Rick Prime is
basically the Rickest Rick in all existence

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:45.700
and seems to be the embodiment of that pessimistic
view of nihilism that our Rick used to follow.

00:11:45.700 --> 00:11:48.940
Out of what little we've seen of him Rick
Prime genuinely doesn't seem to care about

00:11:48.940 --> 00:11:53.630
anything beyond spreading the good word of
not caring about anything to his fellow Rick's.

00:11:53.630 --> 00:11:59.450
He doesn't even seem to care about his family
at all.

00:11:59.450 --> 00:12:05.589
Basically, Rick Prime is like the Rick that
we knew from previous seasons but amped up

00:12:05.589 --> 00:12:10.300
to 11 without any of the character development,
growth and healing that we've seen our Rick

00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:11.300
going through.

00:12:11.300 --> 00:12:14.760
He's the sort of guy that would hop to a new
universe in a heartbeat, leaving all his loved

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:16.940
ones behind the second things get dicey.

00:12:16.940 --> 00:12:20.790
So just like the space dinosaurs floated through
the universe, helping out anyone they could.

00:12:20.790 --> 00:12:24.860
Rick Prime goes from university universe,
making things worse through the creation of

00:12:24.860 --> 00:12:27.130
other pessimistic, nihilistic Ricks.

00:12:27.130 --> 00:12:30.570
But in that process he literally created our
Rick.

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:34.370
He killed his wife and daughter, which sets
off the events of the series, including the

00:12:34.370 --> 00:12:38.400
healing and character growth that we're watching
our Rick go through right now.

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:42.550
And through this process, our Rick is taking
his first steps to being Rick Prime's equal

00:12:42.550 --> 00:12:45.160
opposite, a Rick that genuinely cares.

00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:47.000
You know the status quo of this franchise?

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:50.530
The irreverent adventures where nothing of
any consequence happens.

00:12:50.530 --> 00:12:52.300
That is a thing of the past.

00:12:52.300 --> 00:12:54.340
Represented in a lot of ways by Rick Prime.

00:12:54.340 --> 00:12:58.380
Just think, we finally know what happened
to our Rick's family; when it happened on

00:12:58.380 --> 00:13:01.699
the timeline, where it happened in the multiverse,
how it went down and who did it.

00:13:01.699 --> 00:13:03.930
But we're still missing that one key piece
of data,

00:13:03.930 --> 00:13:04.930
Why?

00:13:04.930 --> 00:13:07.230
Why did Rick Prime do this in the first place?

00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:11.300
Explicitly all we get is a vague, offhanded
explanation that Rick's don't typically refuse

00:13:11.300 --> 00:13:15.501
the call to multiversal adventure, but we
don't know why it's so important to Rick Prime

00:13:15.501 --> 00:13:17.490
that other Rick's follow in his footsteps.

00:13:17.490 --> 00:13:19.279
Maybe this is why.

00:13:19.279 --> 00:13:23.810
Maybe he understands that an equally powerful
but oppositely aligned Rick would one day

00:13:23.810 --> 00:13:25.140
rise up to stop him.

00:13:25.140 --> 00:13:27.860
And he wanted to make sure that that didn't
happen.

00:13:27.860 --> 00:13:31.590
Or maybe fate is just pulling him to create
his equal opposite Rick.

00:13:31.590 --> 00:13:34.180
I, for one, can't wait to find out.

